WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:19.270
Please watch the entire video before caring
for your incision.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:24.710
This video will teach you how to care for
your incision.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.730
An incision is a cut made in your skin during
an operation.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:39.570
The edges of your incision may be held together
with stitches, staples, skin glue or skin

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.220
closure strips.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.399
A bandage called a “dressing” covers your
incision.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:51.210
To prevent skin infections, keep your dressing
clean and dry.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:56.269
Taking good care of your incision will help
it heal more quickly.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:04.280
Caring for your incision involves cleaning
your incision and changing your dressing.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:13.060
You will need: new bandages, a roll of medical
tape, clean scissors, two pairs of disposable

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:24.880
gloves, two clean, soft cloths or paper towels,
a disposable plastic bag, and a waste basket.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.930
Keep these materials away from your incision
to reduce your risk of infection.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:36.630
Prepare Materials
Step One: Wash your hands with soap and water

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:42.770
for 30-seconds and then dry them with a clean
towel or paper towels.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.680
Clean hands are the best protection against
spreading infection.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.750
Step Two: Open a new bandage wrapper.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:55.890
Leave the bandage inside the wrapper and be
careful not to touch it.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:02.640
Step Three: Cut four pieces of tape and hang
them from the edge of a nearby table or counter.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:10.259
The pieces of tape should be the same length
as the old ones around your incision.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.270
Remove Old Dressing
Step One: Wash your hands again with soap

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.930
and water, and then dry them.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.230
Step Two: Put on disposable gloves.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.920
Step Three: With one hand, press down gently
on the skin next to the old tape around your

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.319
incision.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:36.620
With the other hand, carefully peel away the
old tape toward your incision.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:45.390
Step Four: After loosening the tape, remove
your old dressing.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:49.519
Do not remove any skin closure strips covering
your incision.

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:54.380
Let them fall off on their own in about 5
to 10 days.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:58.569
If your dressing sticks to your incision,
don’t tear it off.

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:02.140
Tearing it off may cause bleeding and delay
healing.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.890
Moisten the dressing with warm water.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.569
Wait for a minute or two until you can easily
remove it.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:11.870
Look at your old dressing after your remove
it.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:17.799
If the dressing has a foul or sweet smell
or it has yellow or greenish stain, call your

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:20.650
healthcare provider.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:27.080
Step Five: Place the old dressing in a disposable
plastic bag.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.000
Step Six: Remove the disposable gloves.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:37.510
Throw the gloves and the plastic bag containing
your old dressing in the waste basket.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.610
Clean the Incision
Clean your incision only if your healthcare

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.020
provider says you should.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:49.269
Your healthcare provider may only want you
change your dressing.

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:54.140
Make sure you understand what your healthcare
provider wants you to do, and be sure to call

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.550
with questions or concerns.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:02.650
Step One: Wash your hands with soap and water
and dry them.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.549
Step Two: Put on a new pair of disposable
gloves.

00:04:07.549 --> 00:04:13.220
Step Three: Always use a clean cloth, warm
water, and if your healthcare provider says

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.500
you can, a mild antibacterial soap.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.940
To gently remove dried material from the area
around your incision, start with the areas

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:28.300
nearest your incision and wipe outwards or
away from the incision.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.160
Use a different part of the cloth each time
you wipe your skin.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.120
When the cloth is dirty, use a new, clean
cloth.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.250
Do not scrub or remove any scabs.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:43.300
Scabs protect your incision while it heals.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:46.449
Allow them to fall off on their own.

00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:53.060
Do not wipe or rub over stitches, staples,
or skin closure strips.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:05:00.620
Unless your healthcare provider says you should,
do not clean your incision with alcohol, iodine,

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:05.560
or peroxide.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.080
Step Four: Let your incision air dry.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:17.669
As it dries, check your incision for the following:
stitches or staples that are out of place,

00:05:17.669 --> 00:05:26.690
the edges of your incision pulling open, bleeding
around your incision, redness that extends

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:35.830
more than one inch from your incision edges,
increased skin warmth around your incision,

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:45.930
swelling around your incision, foul or sweet
smelling incision odor, or a white, yellow,

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:50.930
or green discharge coming from your incision.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:57.050
Call your healthcare provider if you notice
any of these changes.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:04.360
Unless your healthcare provider says you should,
do not use ointment, lotion, or powder on

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.710
your incision.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:15.539
Step Five: Remove your disposable gloves and
throw them in the waste basket.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:22.139
Apply New Dressing
Step One: Wash your hands with soap and water

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:25.020
and dry them.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.870
Step Two: Put on a new pair of disposable
gloves.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.780
Step Three: Pick up a piece of gauze by one
corner.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.479
Do not touch the side that will be next to
your incision.

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:42.490
Carefully cover your incision with the gauze
pad.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:50.710
You may need more than one gauze pad if fluid
is still coming out of your incision.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.860
Hold the gauze pad in place with one hand
and tape the gauze pad to your skin with the

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.699
other hand.

00:06:57.699 --> 00:07:02.580
Make sure you seal all four edges of the dressing.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:07.340
If your skin is red from the tape on your
old dressing, put the new tape on a different

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:13.050
part of your skin.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:18.069
If your skin is swollen or has blisters, contact
your healthcare provider.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:23.629
You may be allergic to the tape and need to
try a different kind.

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:29.800
Step Five: Remove your gloves and put them
into the waste basket.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:35.289
Step Six: Wash your hands again with soap
and water and dry them.

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:43.259
It’s normal to see mild redness and swelling
around your incision edges or a clear or pinkish

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:50.699
discharge; however, you should contact your
healthcare provider if you notice any white,

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:57.950
yellow, or green discharge coming from your
incision, a foul or sweet incision odor, the

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:04.050
shape of your incision changes, redness that
extends more than one inch from your incision

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:12.810
edges, increased warmth of the incision, and
bleeding or swelling around your incision.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:23.099
Also, call your healthcare provider if you
have nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or a temperature

00:08:23.099 --> 00:08:26.120
of 101-degrees Fahrenheit or higher.

